commentBody:
@Francis
'Bout time.
I've been relying on Matt/Spring Hill to speak my piece but I remain as disappointed as others about the Great Easening.

@Ιασων

“I would argue that creating difficulties by obscure trivia is the sign of a frustrated setter/editor.”

I’d have to argue otherwise. *Judicious* usage of obscure trivia is a totally valid way to add difficulty to a puzzle (for a presumptive majority of solvers). It just needs to be incorporated in such a way that solvers can get the trivia answers without knowing them.

@RozzieGrandma

Yeah, what Steve L said.

But I got all but one letter on crosses, and the final letter came before my understanding of the clue and answer.

@Vic

When they're rolled, I suppose? 
My yoga mat is rolled, so that’s what came to mind.

@Francis

I agree with you… except for the NE section, which had me all kinds of messed up. First of all, I had “Bikram yoga” instead of HOT PILATES and “ere” instead of TIL. I didn’t know the suffragist, I know nothing about bitcoin, and although I had SWEET, “IE PIE” just didn’t occur to me. 

It’s frustrating when all your insufficiencies occur in the same area rather than being spread across the puzzle, but that’s just the way it goes sometimes.

@SP

There is definitely a Great Easening going on. I'm liking your puzzles on Crosshare more and more. You don't patronize the solver.

I have a feeling the NYT editors may be more to blame than the constructors. I don't know how much they mess with the constructors' cluing but I assume it's a significant amount. I've been finding the clues to be very "homogenized" recently rather than challenging.

@john ezra

John, it's so very nice that you have such a high opinion of me, but calling me a Saint is really quite an overstatement.  Angelic, at best.  

And I don't remember any of that ever happening to me, so maybe my memory is failing as well.  I'm frail, for sure, and I don't see as well as I used to, but "sightless" is  really a bit beyond the pale.

@Francis

Remembrance of past lives is often kept behind the curtain.
Do you ever find yourself talking to the birds?

@Al  in Pittsburgh

Not really.  I tried to start something with a mynah bird, but it just kept mocking me by repeating everything I said.  Very maddening.

Then for a while I had my eye on a Norwegian Blue.  It had beautiful plumage, but turned out to be dead.

@Bill

😂  She should have gone for the more expensive subscription.

I'm telling you, this tier model is getting completely out of hand.

@RandallP

All that rings a bell.

I know it's TINnitus, but why isn't the joint ache problem called ARTHritis?

@Francis,

I’ve had my own tinnitus problems over the years. I’ve found that when I think about it, I can hear the ringing most clearly. But if I’m distracted or forget about it, it fades away enough not to bother me. 

Clearly a brain thing. Maybe that’s not entirely bad.

@NYC Traveler
@sandi

Aw, I'm probably just feeling more sorry for myself than usual tonight.  I really honestly shouldn't complain.

As far as the brain thing...I see your point, but my brain is what I tend to worry about the most.  What if it suddenly decides to obsess on that that frequency distribution?  How would I deal with that?  Or sometimes when it seems worse than other times, I wonder if this is the new normal?

In other words, tinnitus, and maybe the brain itself, seems almost medievally-poorly understood--which I find disturbing in and of itself.

@RandallP
@SP

Thank you for confirming my suspicion the clue wasn't quite right! I remember reading that the causes of tinnitus are not quite clear and research is ongoing.

@Francis

I wager it would be quite embarrassing if I asked you to join me, too!

(For both of us)

@Andrzej
@Francis

I'd pay good money to watch that.

@Matt

Thankfully I don't need it

(But I'm tempted just for lulz)

@Jaclyn Herrin

Is the scripture still called The Book of Mormon?

Will I still have to go off campus to get a cup of coffee while I'm attending a conference at BYU?

@Petrol I appreciate your respectful response. Honestly I’m always happy to see the word in the grid as well as whenever Utah, Utes, Provo, Orem, and Ogden show up as answers (and that seems to happen a lot!). These words and their vowels contribute to excellent play, and as a state so far away from New York it’s fun to be included!

Even though Utah is far away, it is interesting to note the significant ties the church has to New York. New York is the foundational birthplace for the restoration of the church's theology and priesthood authority. Joseph Smith was led to the plates in Palmyra, and the first publication of Book of Mormon was in New York. So being included feels quite appropriate.

There is no problem with MORMON being used as an answer, but clues could maybe be adjusted to better reflect respect and understanding.

@Beth in Greenbelt  I completely understand that people have very different experiences with the Church, and I respect your right to feel that way. 

My question wasn’t about theology or whether individuals are obligated to use preferred terminology, it was about why a respected editorial institution like the NYT would consciously choose a term that the group itself has specifically asked the press not to use.

So there are two very different questions; whether someone personally accepts that request is a different question from whether a newspaper should consider it when making editorial choices.

@Jaclyn Herrin

No offense, but I find all your replies in this thread to be thoughtful, empathetic and polite.

(Who here doubted I actually meant 'No offense'?)

@Jaclyn Herrin

What I want to know is, do their kids play with LEGO or LEGOS?

(Longtime Wordplayers will understand the analogy.)

@Cyndie

They use "LDS" as both the name of the religion and as an adjective describing adherents.

As with "American Indian," said adherents may or may not care which term is used.

@Lewis

Actually, yes!
(somebody ought to write a song in protesr about that 😉)

@Whoa Nellie
